{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:f4cbfc58a683894a66b028420969bb75a35fe4acda1bd3dce0024062c11b2041", "sample_idx": 39, "model_name": "meta/llama-3.2-3b-instruct", "content": "\"Readers of The Guardian can access the print version of a unique Sudoku puzzle, which deviates from traditional Sudoku rules. The puzzle presents a series of numbers placed within a grid, under specific constraints that must be adhered to in order to solve it. These rules create a distinctive challenge, requiring players to think creatively and strategically. The puzzle is available for purchase in the next week's Observer Digital Edition.\"\n\nAddition to the standard Sudoku rules, this puzzle presents a unique twist. The addition of grey lines to the grid serves to further complicate matters, as the numbers contained within these lines must add up to the figures in the corner. This requirement adds an extra layer of complexity, making the puzzle more engaging and challenging for those who enjoy Sudoku.\n\nThe puzzle's design also includes a restriction that no number can be repeated within each shape formed by the grey lines. This constraint ensures that players must think carefully and methodically, as they try to fill in each cell with the correct number. The combination of these rules creates a stimulating and intellectually taxing puzzle that is sure to appeal to fans of Sudoku.\n\nThe unique nature of this puzzle creates an intriguing challenge that is sure to capture the attention of those who enjoy puzzle-solving. With its distinctive rules and intricate design, it offers a refreshing take on the traditional Sudoku experience. By incorporating the grey lines and corner sum requirements, the puzzle's creators have managed to create a truly original and engaging puzzle that is sure to delight.\n\nIn order to fully appreciate the puzzle, readers are encouraged to purchase the next week's Observer Digital Edition, which will feature the completed puzzle. The comp